Database: SDD

Purpose:
  Database technology catalog with comparison criteria, selection rules,
  and compatibility requirements. Root stack/ defines reusable database
  knowledge; projects/{project_name}/stack/ stores the selected database for the project.

Catalog:
  PostgreSQL:
    Type: Relational
    Pros:
      - ACID compliance
      - Rich data types (JSON, arrays, ranges)
      - Excellent query planner
      - Strong consistency
      - Extensible (PostGIS, pgvector)
    Cons:
      - Higher operational complexity than SQLite
      - Vertical scaling limits
    BestFor:
      - Complex relational data
      - ACID requirements
      - JSON alongside relational
      - Geospatial data
    AvoidWhen:
      - Simple key-value (prefer Redis)
      - Document-heavy (prefer MongoDB)
    Performance:
      - Excellent with proper indexing
      - MVCC overhead
    Ecosystem:
      - Very large
    Hiring:
      - Widely available

  MySQL:
    Type: Relational
    Pros:
      - Widely deployed
      - Simple replication
      - Large ecosystem
      - Good read performance
    Cons:
      - Less feature-rich than PostgreSQL
      - Weaker JSON support
    BestFor:
      - Simple relational data
      - Read-heavy workloads
      - WordPress-style applications
    AvoidWhen:
      - Complex queries
      - JSON-heavy schemas
    Performance:
      - Good for simple queries
    Ecosystem:
      - Very large
    Hiring:
      - Widely available

  MongoDB:
    Type: Document
    Pros:
      - Flexible schema
      - Horizontal scaling
      - Document-oriented
      - Aggregation pipeline
    Cons:
      - No ACID across documents
      - Memory-hungry
      - Data duplication
    BestFor:
      - Unstructured data
      - Rapid prototyping
      - Content management
    AvoidWhen:
      - Complex transactions
      - Relational data
    Performance:
      - Good for document workloads
    Ecosystem:
      - Large
    Hiring:
      - Moderate availability

  Redis:
    Type: Key-Value / Cache
    Pros:
      - In-memory speed
      - Rich data structures
      - Pub/Sub
      - Lua scripting
    Cons:
      - Memory-limited
      - Not for large datasets
    BestFor:
      - Caching
      - Session storage
      - Rate limiting
      - Pub/Sub messaging
    AvoidWhen:
      - Primary data store
      - Large datasets
    Performance:
      - Excellent (in-memory)
    Ecosystem:
      - Very large
    Hiring:
      - Widely available

  SQLite:
    Type: Embedded Relational
    Pros:
      - Zero configuration
      - Single file
      - ACID compliant
      - Embedded
    Cons:
      - No network access
      - Limited concurrency
      - No built-in replication
    BestFor:
      - Prototypes
      - Edge devices
      - Simple applications
      - Testing
    AvoidWhen:
      - High-concurrency production
      - Distributed systems
    Performance:
      - Excellent for local access
    Ecosystem:
      - Large
    Hiring:
      - Widely available

SelectionRules:
  - RequirementFit: data model requirements
  - Consistency: ACID requirements
  - Scale: read/write patterns and volume
  - QueryComplexity: query patterns and complexity
  - OperationalFit: team expertise and operational burden
  - Cost: licensing and infrastructure cost
  - Compatibility: integration with other stack components

TechnologyScore:
  RequirementFit: 0-25
  Consistency: 0-20
  Scale: 0-15
  QueryComplexity: 0-10
  OperationalFit: 0-10
  Cost: 0-10
  Compatibility: 0-10

State: +
  